BUILD AND RUN PROGRAMS ON PICONIX

PicoNIX has small on-device programs, NOT a desktop toolchain.
The compilers write short files in RAM FS; the generated binary
is always /tmp/a.out (PNX1 header + Thumb machine code).

C pipeline:
  cc /tmp/hello.c       # writes /tmp/hello.s
  as /tmp/hello.s       # writes /tmp/a.out
  /tmp/a.out            # run only after both succeed

Direct assembly:
  as /tmp/blink.s
  /tmp/a.out

Python-like scripts:
  python /tmp/check.py

Use MCP write_file for a source; it overwrites target /tmp path.
Source must be ASCII for write_file. C input <=1024 B, generated
assembly <=4096 B internally, but as source input <=2048 B.
Some legal C source can therefore generate assembly too large
for as. Simplify the code. Assembler output <=2040 code bytes.
Python input <=2048 B with <=64 nonempty program lines.

Do not use #include, import, malloc, regular Python modules or
GNU build commands unless a particular interpreter documents them.
Compilation errors are usually 'unsupported C syntax or output too
large', or 'invalid instruction, label or size'. See language pages.

Execution slots: 6 slots x 24 KiB, 4 KiB per-task stack reserved;
not all of 24 KiB is available for code/data. Applications built
into the image are different from code generated on the device.

Verification pattern:
  1. Verify file exists: ls /tmp
  2. Run cc/as; inspect their output and return status.
  3. Execute /tmp/a.out; compare stdout to expected result.
  4. If unexpected, inspect assembly or use trace on/show.
  5. Never assume a previous /tmp/a.out is the new build.
See /man/c-examples, /man/asm-examples, /man/python-examples.
